*(
    title: Disease
    description: The branch where the rest of the course is put to work, and how its seven internotes divide into two halves.
    tags: disease, branch overview, infection, immunity, epidemiology, biology
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Disease is the branch that uses everything else. A pathogen is an organism exploiting another organism's cells and systems, immunity is those systems responding, and non-infectious disease is what happens when they fail with nothing having invaded.

    Almost nothing in this branch is a new idea. The membrane, enzymes, natural selection, homeostasis and gene expression all reappear here doing recognisable work, and the branch is largely an exercise in recognising them.

    ===

    It divides cleanly in two, and the division is the one on the canvas: whether anything is transmitted.

    Infectious disease has an agent that moves between hosts, so there is a chain to break and a defence to mount. Everything about how it is prevented, treated and studied follows from those two facts.

    ===

    Non-infectious disease has no agent. Nothing is caught, so nothing can be quarantined and nobody can be vaccinated — and the causes are usually several acting together over decades rather than one acting over days.

    That is why the two halves are studied so differently, and why the same tools do not work on both.
}[
    canvas.diagram{
        box#q{
            Is anything transmitted?
            the question that splits the branch
        }

        cue.draw{
            box#inf{
                Infectious
                an agent, a chain between hosts, and a defence
            }
            box#route{
                So: break the chain, or make the host immune
                and study it by tracing transmission
            }
            arrow(from: #q; to: #inf)
            arrow(from: #inf; to: #route)
        }(
            in: 1
        )

        cue.draw{
            box#non{
                Non-infectious
                no agent, and usually several causes over decades
            }
            box#prev{
                So: change the exposures, or replace the lost function
                and study it by comparing large groups
            }
            arrow(from: #q; to: #non)
            arrow(from: #non; to: #prev)
        }(
            in: 2
        )
    }(
        flow: right
        title: Two halves, and why they need different tools
    )
]

scene{
    # The route through it

    The infectious half runs in a natural order. !internote(ref: causes-of-infectious-disease.chalk; !connect) covers the agents themselves, how each is transmitted, and the standard for showing that a particular microbe causes a particular disease. !internote(ref: responses-to-pathogens.chalk; !connect) is what the host does about it — the barriers and the innate response, which between them stop almost everything, and the plant defences that work with no circulating immune system at all.

    !internote(ref: immunity.chalk; !connect) is the adaptive response, slow once and fast forever after, which is the asymmetry every vaccine depends on. !internote(ref: prevention-treatment-and-control.chalk; !connect) then sets out the interventions: hygiene and quarantine, vaccination and herd immunity, antibiotics and antivirals, and the resistance that keeps eroding them. !internote(ref: epidemiology.chalk; !connect) is the discipline that measures whether any of it works, and where the limits of what a study can establish are set.

    The non-infectious half is shorter. !internote(ref: non-infectious-disease.chalk; !connect) covers the causes — genetic, environmental and nutritional — how they interact, and why these conditions now account for most deaths in Australia. !internote(ref: technologies-and-disorders.chalk; !connect) is what can be done when the biology cannot be repaired: replacing a lost function with a device, for hearing, sight and kidney failure.
}[
    table{
        Half            | Internote                          | The question it answers
        Infectious      | Causes of Infectious Disease        | what kinds of agent are there, and how is causation established?
        Infectious      | Responses to Pathogens              | what stops most infections before you ever feel ill?
        Infectious      | Immunity                            | why is the second exposure different from the first?
        Infectious      | Prevention, Treatment and Control   | which link of the chain does each measure attack?
        Infectious      | Epidemiology                        | what can a study of populations establish, and what can it not?
        Non-infectious  | Non-infectious Disease              | what causes disease when nothing is transmitted?
        Non-infectious  | Technologies and Disorders          | what can a device do when the biology has failed?
    }(
        title: Seven internotes, in reading order
        highlight rows: 7, 8
        highlight colour: orange
    )
]

scene{
    ## What to carry out of it

    The first thing is that antibiotic resistance is not a special process. It is the four conditions from *Natural Selection* operating on a bacterial population with a generation time of twenty minutes, and every piece of standard advice about antibiotics follows from that mechanism rather than from convention.

    ===

    The second is that a fever is homeostasis working, not failing. The hypothalamus moves its own set point, and the body then works towards the new one — which is why a person with a fever shivers. Recognising the loop from !internote(ref: organisms.chalk; !connect) inside an unfamiliar situation is most of what this branch asks for.

    The third is about evidence. Koch's postulates are a high standard that many important diseases cannot meet, and epidemiology is a lower standard that can identify a cause well enough to act on decades before anyone can say why. John Snow stopped a cholera outbreak thirty years before the bacterium responsible could be grown.

    Knowing what each kind of evidence can and cannot establish is worth more, in this branch, than any individual fact in it.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        axis.label(x: 0; label: first exposure; line; colour: grey)
        axis.label(x: 30; label: second exposure; line; colour: grey)
        curve(f: 100 * exp(-((x - 12)/5)^2); domain: [0, 28]; colour: blue; label: primary response)

        cue{
            curve(f: 900 * exp(-((x - 37)/5)^2); domain: [30, 60]; colour: orange; label: secondary response)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        x axis label: days
        y axis label: antibody in the blood
        x domain: [0, 60]
        y domain: [0, 1000]
        !show ticks
    )
]
